    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d130">

                <pb facs="0239" n="163" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg163"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">660F.116/126: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Czechoslovakia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wright</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Prague</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-12-22T11:00:00+01:00">December 22, 1934—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 22—10:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>75. Department’s telegram No. 47, December 19, 7 [<hi rendition="#italic">6</hi>]
                    p.m. The Ministerial decree is based upon new Czechoslovak-German Clearing <pb
                        facs="0240" n="164" xml:id="pg164"/> and Continuance [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">sic</hi>] Agreement reported by Commercial Attaché in
                    special report No. 74, November [28th], text of which was forwarded in same mail
                    December 1st.<anchor n="30" xml:id="d130-f1"/> The Governor of Czechoslovak
                    National Bank states that deduction of 4 percent is based upon arbitrary
                    assumption by Czech and German authorities that commission port charges will
                    equal that amount and that the arrangement is applicable to all shipments of
                    cotton via German ports irrespective of whether negotiated directly or through
                    German agents.</p>
                <p>In transactions where the agent in Germany signs the invoices it would seem that
                    the two countries are within their rights in this regard but any agreement
                    affecting the payments on commercial contracts with a third country wherein
                    German interest does not appear seems questionable from ethical and legal
                    standpoints.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wright</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
